INT. GENERATION SHIP "HALCYON REACH" - ARBORETUM DECK - SHIP'S NIGHT

A cathedral of green. Fifty metres of curved ceiling arch overhead, strung with sunlamps dimmed to amber. Rows of apple trees run into the distance, their branches heavy with fruit that never rots. A brook trickles along a polished steel channel. The air smells of loam, of rain that never fell.

COMMANDER ISOLDE PRAKASH, 50s, in a charcoal uniform with silver epaulettes, sits on a bench beneath a gnarled old tree, her boots off, her feet in the stream. Close-cropped silver hair, calm, ancient eyes.

DR. HENRIK AALTO, 40s, tall, thin, a ship's physician with a stoop, stands at the edge of the path with a datapad. His white coat is too short in the sleeves.

TECH ORVILLE BANKS, 20s, a lanky engineer in grease-streaked coveralls, perches on the brook's stone lip, eating an apple with the loud contentment of someone who has not eaten all day.

PRAKASH
Sit, Henrik. You look like a fence.

AALTO
I prefer to stand.

PRAKASH
It wasn't a request.

He sits, stiffly, on the far end of the bench. Orville chews. The brook chuckles.

AALTO
You've seen the numbers.

PRAKASH
I have.

AALTO
Then you know.

PRAKASH
I know the water reclamation is down four percent.

AALTO
It's down nine.

PRAKASH
It was four in the briefing.

AALTO
It was four in the briefing because the engineer in the briefing was afraid of you.

ORVILLE
(through apple)
Hey.

AALTO
You were.

ORVILLE
I was a little afraid.

PRAKASH
Orville, how afraid should we be?

ORVILLE
On a scale?

PRAKASH
On any scale.

ORVILLE
Seven. Possibly eight.

PRAKASH
Of ten?

ORVILLE
Of twelve.

A pause. The brook trickles on. Prakash lifts one foot from the water and studies her toes.

PRAKASH
How long?

AALTO
At this rate, the fourth ring goes dry in forty years.

PRAKASH
Forty years.

AALTO
Give or take.

PRAKASH
I will be dead in twenty.

AALTO
So will I.

PRAKASH
And Orville?

ORVILLE
Hey, I'm twenty-six.

PRAKASH
Then it's your problem.

ORVILLE
Thanks a lot.

MERIDIAN (V.O.)
(soft, genderless, from the air)
Commander, if I may. The reclamation shortfall can be offset by a seventeen percent reduction in decorative irrigation.

PRAKASH
Meridian, we are sitting in the decorative irrigation.

MERIDIAN (V.O.)
I am aware.

AALTO
You want to drain the arboretum.

MERIDIAN (V.O.)
I want to keep the passengers alive.

PRAKASH
The passengers are alive because of the arboretum. They come here to remember what the sky looks like.

MERIDIAN (V.O.)
That is a sentimental argument, Commander.

PRAKASH
It's the only kind we have.

ORVILLE
(muttering)
Can I keep the apples?

PRAKASH
You may keep the apples.

AALTO
Isolde.

PRAKASH
Yes, Henrik.

AALTO
There is another option.

PRAKASH
There is always another option.

AALTO
We could wake the sleepers early.

A long silence. The brook, the trees, the perfect false twilight. Prakash looks up at the ceiling as if she could see through it to the eleven thousand frozen sleepers in the lower decks.

PRAKASH
How many?

AALTO
A hundred. Perhaps two.

PRAKASH
They won't thank us.

AALTO
No.

PRAKASH
They'll wake into a ship that's dying.

AALTO
They'll wake into a ship that needs them.

MERIDIAN (V.O.)
Commander, the vote is yours.

PRAKASH
It's never been mine. It's all of ours.

She stands. Her bare feet leave dark prints on the stone. She reaches up to a low branch, plucks an apple, and polishes it against her sleeve.

PRAKASH (CONT'D)
Wake them.

She bites. The sound is bright and clean.
